.TH RUSTYMUSICSHEETS 1 "November 2026" "RustyMusicSheets 0.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rustymusicsheets \- sheet music library and performance reader
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rustymusicsheets
.RI [ option ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
RustyMusicSheets is a native Linux application for organizing PDF/PNG/JPEG sheet music,
watching a music folder, creating setlists, and viewing scores during performance.
With no arguments it opens the desktop interface.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display available commands.
.TP
.BI "\-\-import " PATH
Import a PDF/PNG/JPEG or recursively import supported scores from a folder.
Return a nonzero exit status if any files fail.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List scores in the library.
.TP
.BI "\-\-detect\-instruments " [ID]
Create offline instrument proposals for all scores, or the specified score ID.
Uses PDF text extraction first, then Tesseract OCR when needed.
Review and apply proposals in Library options; this command does not assign them.
.TP
.BI "\-\-omr " "SOURCE OUTPUT_DIR"
Recognize printed music using the optional Audiveris executable on Linux.
Use
.BI "\-\-audiveris " EXE
to override its executable path/name, without shell arguments.
Print the generated MusicXML movement paths; do not assume a fixed output filename.
.TP
.BI "\-\-inspect\-musicxml " FILE
Print JSON describing MusicXML parts, tempos, warnings, and original-page
references when available. Accepts compressed .mxl and plain .musicxml/.xml.
.TP
.BI "\-\-midi " "MUSICXML OUTPUT.mid"
Export a new Standard MIDI File from supported MusicXML.
Existing output files are not overwritten. The following options apply:
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-parts " P1,P2
Select MusicXML part IDs. Omit to select all; use inspection output for actual IDs.
.TP
.BI "\-\-program " P1=43
Override a part's one-based General MIDI instrument (1\-128).
May be repeated for different parts. Piano is 1; cello is 43.
.TP
.BI "\-\-tempo\-scale " FACTOR
Multiply the musical tempo; supported range is 0.1\-4.0, default 1.0.
.TP
.B \-\-play
Play exported MIDI through the external FluidSynth executable.
.TP
.BI "\-\-play\-seconds " SECONDS
Stop playback after 0.1\-3600 seconds. Requires \-\-play.
Without this limit, playback continues until the file finishes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-soundfont " FILE
Choose the .sf2/.sf3 sound bank used for CLI playback.
.TP
.BI "\-\-fluidsynth " EXE
Override the CLI synth executable path/name.
.RE
.PP
The three music commands above use explicit files/directories without opening
the library database. CLI engines use defaults/arguments, not saved GUI preferences.
CLI playback is one-shot; it does not expose the GUI transport or page following.
CLI MIDI reads the supplied notation without GUI part combinations or saved
mixes. Use Export selected MIDI in the GUI for a combined layout.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Check availability of Poppler PDF helpers and Tesseract.
This does not check Audiveris, FluidSynth, SoundFonts or all OCR language data.
.TP
.B \-\-smoke\-test
Open the desktop interface and close it after three seconds.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directory for the metadata database and default managed score storage.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for saved preferences.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Base directory reserved for disposable application cache data.
.TP
.B TESSDATA_PREFIX
Optional OCR language-data directory inherited by the external helpers.
Audiveris may need full compatible Tesseract models rather than distro
LSTM-only models. Configure its own language data separately when needed.
.SH FILES
.I ~/.local/share/rustymusicsheets/
.br
.I ~/.config/rustymusicsheets/preferences.json
.PP
A selected music folder stores score files separately from application metadata.
GUI recognition artifacts/logs, result indexes and saved movement mixes live
under the data directory in omr/, omr-index/ and omr-mix/.
Back up the complete data/config directories and original music folder with
the application closed; a SQLite metadata backup is not a score backup.
.SH RELEASE AND REQUIREMENTS
The v0.5.0 preview is published at:
.UR https://github.com/ripleyeldridge/rustymusicsheets/releases/tag/v0.5.0
v0.5.0 release notes and downloads
.UE
.PP
The amd64 Linux package requires glibc >=2.43 and is not compatible with
Ubuntu 22.04/24.04. Rebuild on the target distribution rather than replacing glibc.
Stable/latest remains v0.2.1; v0.5.0 is a prerelease.
.PP
The published .deb includes the SDK-enabled helper at
/usr/libexec/rustymusicsheets/rms-audio-host. APT resolves system runtime
libraries; no compiler or VST3 SDK is needed to run it.
Audiveris, the external FluidSynth executable for legacy playback, banks,
plugins and sample libraries remain separate. Host playback requires existing
JACK or PipeWire-JACK stereo output; the app does not configure an audio server.
.PP
The separate ARM64 Android APK is experimental, debuggable and locally
debug-signed, not production signed or desktop feature parity. API 26 minimum,
target API 36; the v0.5.0 APK passed static checks but was not executed on a device.
Preserve data and signer continuity; do not uninstall to bypass a mismatch.
Release checksums, unsigned build provenance and third-party notices are
attached on GitHub. Attachments use flat filenames; checksums are not signatures.
.SH MUSIC AND PERFORMANCE
Install Audiveris separately for optional Linux recognition.
Default legacy playback needs the external FluidSynth executable and a bank.
The optional audio-host helper links the system FluidSynth library, but
pure-VST3 host playback needs neither that executable nor a SoundFont bank.
Instrument-label detection is text classification, not note transcription.
Audiveris is AGPLv3, FluidSynth LGPL, and SoundFonts have separate licenses.
.PP
Open Reader's Music / OMR window, or Performance's Accompaniment strip.
Recognize music when needed; saved recognition loads automatically in Performance.
Multiple exported movements require an explicit choice.
Browse controls choose engine, SoundFont, MusicXML input and export paths.
Input/output selection fills a field; load/export/save actions remain explicit.
Cancellation preserves fields; exports do not overwrite existing files.
.PP
Play/Pause, Rewind 5s, Fast-forward 5s and Stop control the persistent GUI synth.
Pause freezes position; resume/seek reconstruct held notes and restart envelopes.
Stop resets musical position, not the displayed page.
Parts / volume controls independent mute, instrument and levels. Legacy
playback/MIDI support up to 15 pitched parts; the native host supports up to 32.
Both staves of a piano part stay together.
Master volume scales all instruments without changing their balance.
Master gain affects playback output, not exported MIDI part levels.
Mix settings are saved per recognized movement.
.PP
Combine parts reunites explicitly selected OMR fragments at their existing
absolute times. Save and unload instruments first; choose a retained target,
name and preview before applying. No automatic abbreviation merges occur.
Uncombine restores original notes/names while retaining subsequent target
instrument settings. Exact-score/MusicXML-bound .audio-v3.json mixes retain
hidden member settings and leave older sidecars untouched.
After failed/cancelled saving, use Reload movement (discard unsaved edits).
Recover using current layout (back up rejected mix) preserves a rejected
sidecar before replacing it. Settings changes require a new preview.
Playback/MIDI use combinations; Export original MusicXML preserves source
bytes and original parts. Finish plugin dialogs before saving/unloading.
.PP
Enable Follow score pages for optional automatic original-score page turning.
Consistent Audiveris source-sheet/page/system metadata is required; page duration
is not guessed and printed page labels are not PDF indexes.
Turn pages early by ... measures accepts an integer from 0\-999, default zero.
It follows played measures through repeats/endings and tempo changes.
Oversized lead is capped to avoid skipping a whole page.
Two-page mode keeps the current spread while the target page is visible.
.PP
Previous/Next page buttons remain available in Performance without the synth.
Manual taps, pedals, page jumps and bookmark navigation disable auto-follow
until re-enabled. Paused seeks can update the page; Stop/completion never
automatically starts another setlist score. Help/Library suspend visual following.
.PP
Recognition and musical performance are unverified approximations: review pitches,
rhythm, ties, repeats, part names and pagination. Expression, pedal, grace notes,
advanced navigation and nested repeats may be unsupported or approximated.
There is no VST2/CLAP/LV2 hosting, microphone score tracking, looping or metronome.
Android OMR/synth parity is not implemented.
.SS OPTIONAL AUDIO-HOST PREVIEW
Legacy FluidSynth remains the default. Explicitly select Audio host preview
in Settings for PipeWire/JACK or native JACK and browse to rms-audio-host.
An empty helper path uses /usr/libexec/rustymusicsheets/rms-audio-host.
PipeWire mode uses /usr/bin/pw-jack; native JACK never starts a server.
Both require existing stereo physical playback ports; the first two ports are
connected automatically. Output-routing selection is not implemented yet.
.PP
The companion provides process supervision, bounded Unix-socket control and a
shared device-clock SoundFont/VST3 mixer for up to 32 parts.
VST3 instruments, scanning, parameters/presets and state require an SDK-enabled
helper. Custom editors use separate X11 windows when available.
Wine installation, prefix management and automatic bridge setup are not provided.
MIDI export and legacy playback retain the 15-pitched-part limit.
Host failure stops audio without closing the reader or silently switching engines.
Press Play explicitly to retry. Only owned processes/private files are cleaned up.
.PP
Build from source with python3 scripts/build-audio-host.py --test.
CMake, a C++17 compiler, pkg-config and JACK, FluidSynth and OpenSSL development
packages are required. Ordinary Cargo/Android builds remain independent.
On Ubuntu the helper build packages are build-essential, cmake, pkg-config,
libjack-jackd2-dev, libfluidsynth-dev and libssl-dev. An existing PipeWire
desktop also needs pipewire-jack; fluid-soundfont-gm provides an example bank.
The runtime FluidSynth package alone does not provide development headers.
These prerequisites are installed manually, never by the application.
Wine, yabridge and the VST3 SDK are not needed for this stage.
These are source-build requirements, not requirements for running the
published v0.5.0 package. The base helper build needs CMake >=3.16;
the VST3 build below additionally needs CMake >=3.25 and libx11-dev.
Build the application with cargo build --locked --release and launch
\&./target/release/rustymusicsheets from the source root. Build the separate
helper as above, Browse to its absolute output path, select a SoundFont
and save settings. An older installed app may lack the backend selector.
The helper output is target/audio-host/rms-audio-host.
Use --core-only --test --build-dir target/audio-host-core for scheduler tests
without JACK/FluidSynth headers; core-only mode creates no live helper.
Debian packaging includes the helper only with scripts/build-deb.py --with-audio-host.
Building a package does not install it. Source builds do not install the
/usr/libexec/ helper; leave the helper path empty only for an installed
helper-enabled package. To disable the preview, select FluidSynth (legacy,
default) and save. Backend changes stop playback; press Play explicitly.
.PP
If the helper is missing or the protocol mismatches, build both binaries from
the same source. If JACK/output ports are unavailable, match the existing
server/backend and enable stereo output. Device/rate/output changes require
fixing routing and retrying explicitly. More than ten seconds of reported
latency or exhausted timing history fails closed. Muting does not unload
SoundFonts/free sample RAM. See the README for a muted real-helper smoke test.
.PP
For VST3 use CMake >=3.25 and libx11-dev. Explicitly acquire the pinned official
SDK with python3 scripts/fetch-vst3-sdk.py, then build the helper with
python3 scripts/build-audio-host.py --test --vst3-sdk "$PWD/target/vst3-sdk".
Normal Cargo/CMake/Android builds do not download the SDK.
Use --build-info to check protocol2/vst3 capability and rebuild the app/helper
together; the older M0 helper used protocol1.
.PP
In application Settings (no score required), or Music / OMR engine settings,
expand VST3 bundle discovery (shared by all parts). Browse the single Direct
\&.vst3 bundle folder and Inspect this bundle, or Add search folder / Browse /
Scan selected roots. Scan each bundle once: all part dropdowns share the
catalogue, but assignments, instances, gain and state remain independent.
Browsing/scanning never assigns a part. Retry this bundle retries quarantine.
Selected bundles are shown read-only per part.
Choose the exact class by vendor/path/ID. Linux .vst3 plugins are bundle
directories containing Contents/x86_64-linux/<bundle-name>.so, not raw Windows
files or loose VST2 DLL/SO files. Use Load instruments paused, then Open editor,
Generic parameters, or a matching .vstpreset while paused/stopped.
Post-plugin gain is separate from GM/CC7. Save plugin assignments and state
before changing instruments; external samples/licenses are not bundled.
Missing plugins never silently become SoundFonts. A VST-only session needs
no SoundFont bank. MIDI export retains GM choices, not plugin audio/state.
.PP
SFZ instruments use a separately acquired native sfizz VST3 sampler, not the
SF2/SF3 SoundFont field. Discover sfizz.vst3 globally and select the stereo
sfizz class, not sfizz-multi. Load instruments paused, Open editor and choose
the .sfz inside sfizz, or load a class-matching .vstpreset. Play at a low master
level, then pause/stop and Save plugin assignments and state.
SFZ definitions, samples and includes remain external and must stay available.
Normal builds do not download/install sfizz; no second SFZ engine or
vendor-specific production state format is added.
.PP
Rebuild both reader and helper for synchronous editor file pickers.
The negotiated editor_keepalive capability on X11 distinguishes a busy
editor callback from a missing heartbeat without acknowledging commands early.
Transport is held until the dialog finishes. Finish/cancel it, or use Stop
audio to cancel the owned helper and retain the previous saved checkpoint.
The normal five-second missing-heartbeat watchdog remains enabled; continuous
editor activity has a separate thirty-minute limit.
.PP
For existing yabridge use generated Linux VST3 wrappers, normally
~/.vst3/yabridge, and run Read-only bridge diagnostics before scanning.
Tool/wrapper-root/Wine-loader paths have Browse controls.
Vendor installation/activation and user-reviewed registration/sync are external.
Wine 9.22 and newer (including 10.x and 11.x) with yabridge <=5.1.1 have
known embedded-editor mouse/coordinate failures, even when audio plays.
Diagnostics, scan results and playback controls show a non-blocking notification,
including Wine 11.22 Staging with yabridge 5.1.1; scans and playback are allowed.
Move the editor to the far left of your desktop / leftmost screen as a workaround;
this may restore mouse interaction but does not establish compatibility.
Missing tools, invalid settings and failed/unparseable probes remain errors.
Previously quarantined bundles need Retry this bundle or an explicit rescan.
This source policy does not retroactively alter the published v0.5.0 package.
Updating only to 5.1.1 is not a fix. Optionally select a verified existing
runtime/profile; no automatic installs, sync/prune or prefix changes occur.
WINELOADER is helper-scoped; WINEPREFIX is not globally overridden.
Version preflight is not individual plugin compatibility certification.
Other runtime/bridge combinations still need plugin-specific validation.
VST3 packages require --with-audio-host --vst3-sdk SDK_PATH so pinned
SDK license/provenance is included. The published v0.5.0 .deb uses this
SDK-enabled variant. Building does not install/publish a package.
.PP
Audible position is an estimate requiring loopback calibration. FluidSynth's
internal 64-frame quantum may delay acoustic onset. Chorus/reverb are disabled
for pause/seek flushing, so sound differs from legacy playback. Each part loads
its own SoundFont instance; test memory, levels and timing before performance use.
.SH ANNOTATIONS
Available in v0.5.0: non-destructive vector ink over original score pages.
Choose Annotate in Reader or Performance for Pen, translucent Highlighter,
Stroke eraser, Hand / Pan, color/thickness, Undo/Redo, Save now and Show ink.
Done restores click-to-turn. A tap makes a dot; erasing removes whole strokes.
Clear page requires confirmation and is undoable within session history.
.PP
Page-relative vectors stay aligned through zoom/scroll/resize and two-page
view. A gesture cannot cross to the adjacent page. Drawing does not also scroll
or turn pages; Hand / Pan permits drag-scrolling. Navigation works between
gestures. Escape leaves annotation mode first. Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Shift+Z/Ctrl+Y and
Ctrl+S apply to ink while tools own input.
.PP
Entering annotation mode disables synth page following; explicitly re-enable
it afterward. Audio can continue. Score identity is verified before editing.
Completed gestures autosave in the background; watch saved/error status.
Closing/switching scores waits for saves and offers retry/cancel/discard
recovery. Forced termination can lose a live or not-yet-acknowledged gesture.
.PP
Page sidecars are in app-data/annotations/, under the score hash, named
<zero-based-page>.ink-v1.json. Back up annotations/ with all app data;
SQLite-only backups omit ink. Original PDF/image files remain unchanged.
Atomic content-token-checked writes reject conflicts. Corrupt/newer sidecars
stay read-only until explicit backed-up recovery. Stale locks fail closed:
close all writers and preserve sidecar/lock before manually resolving them.
Limits: 8 MiB, 200,000 points and 10,000 strokes per page; eight cached pages
and bounded session undo. Unsaved pages are not evicted or silently truncated.
.PP
Mouse and OS-recognized stylus/Surface Pen input use constant width and one
active contact. Pressure, palm rejection, pen buttons and particular hardware
are not guaranteed; no drivers/global input settings are changed.
Android shares the engine for supported images, not native PDF/OMR/audio.
Text/stamps, object moving, pixel erasing and annotated PDF/image export are
deferred. Original score/MusicXML and MIDI exports do not contain drawn ink.
.SH NOTES
Folder watching runs while the application is open.
Use the Library section to choose a music folder and configure readable titles.
F11 toggles fullscreen; Escape closes Combine parts, then an open music
window/mixer before leaving performance mode. Reader page-turn shortcuts
are suspended while the combination editor is open.
Arrow keys and PageUp/PageDown turn pages.
.PP
Settings contains an opt-in keep-awake control to inhibit screen dimming,
idle timeout, and suspend while the application is running.
Inhibitors are released on disable or exit; system policies may override them.
Missing folder-picker starting directories fall back to the user's home.
.PP
The Setlists toolbar button opens management controls for saved setlists
and collections, including rename, duplicate, reorder, add/remove, and
confirmed deletion. Removing a list never removes score PDFs.
These Play actions select reader order; they do not start synth accompaniment.
.PP
Library displays a large central score browser with search and filters.
Selecting a score opens the separate full-width Reader tab.
Options toggles the library sidebar; returning to Library retains search state.
Page-turn shortcuts apply in Reader/Performance, not text editing, choosers,
Help or other active editors.
Dialogs are constrained to the current window and scroll when necessary.
.PP
Settings and metadata reuse the left sidebar in place of Library options.
Explicit Rescan imports files and removes confirmed missing-score records
after creating a metadata backup. Automatic watching does not delete records.
Rescan never deletes PDFs; unavailable folders and backup failures prevent cleanup.
.PP
Instrument metadata supports multiple instruments, subtypes, and parts.
Library options provides detection and evidence review; Settings controls
opening pages and OCR language. English data is included in package dependencies.
Additional languages require corresponding Tesseract language packages.
The instrument filter and search use assigned metadata, not unconfirmed proposals.
Manual choices, including intentionally empty choices, are protected.
Optional detection for new GUI imports fills only clear, unassigned,
unprotected instruments. Bare Alto/Tenor/Bass labels require review.
No files are uploaded or modified by detection.
.PP
PNG and JPEG images are single-page scores; image-sequence assembly is not supported.
Images exceeding safety limits or with invalid contents are rejected.
Existing databases receive a metadata backup before instrument-schema migration.
.PP
Help provides a searchable offline topic tree, keyboard reference, and About
details (application version, author, and release year). F1 opens help;
Escape or Close help returns without losing drafts. Page-turn shortcuts are
suspended in Help; enabled synth page following catches up after returning.
.PP
Instrument detection may take a while for large libraries, especially scans
and images requiring OCR. Warnings appear beside detection controls and before
CLI detection. Background progress and cancellation are available in the GUI.
